window.docContents[188].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Lower-Order-Divergence-Form-Problems--Lower-order-terms-and-coercive-shifts":{"contents":"First- and zeroth-order terms need not preserve coercivity, even when the\nprincipal coefficient matrix is uniformly elliptic. The replacement is a\nGårding inequality: the full form controls the gradient up to a multiple of\nthe  norm. Adding a sufficiently large positive mass term absorbs that\nloss and restores a coercive problem. This is the content of Gårding's\ninequality (1953), which Evans (2010, pp. 318–319) states for \ncoefficients and  (Proposition 9.22) places in the\nfunctional-analytic framework of compact perturbations of coercive operators.\n\nWe make the threshold explicit. Bounds for the first-order\noperators are combined with Young's inequality, the zeroth-order operator is\ncontrolled by its norm, and the resulting constants determine\nthe coercive shift. Every larger shift gives the same uniform coercivity lower\nbound and therefore a unique Lax–Milgram solution.\n\nThe forcing is written in the standard  form\n. In Lean this is a continuous functional assembled from\none base  component and one component for each weak derivative.\n\nA one-dimensional model shows why a shift can be needed. Let .\nOn , consider the operator  in weak form:\n\n\n\nTake , the first Dirichlet eigenfunction of\n on  with eigenvalue . Then\n\n\n\nWhen , the form is positive on every eigenspace and hence\ncoercive on all of . When , the form vanishes on\nthe one-dimensional eigenspace of ; coercivity fails, and the equation\n has no solution unless  (the Fredholm\nalternative). When , the form is negative on that eigenspace.\n\nAdding the mass shift  replaces  by\n\n\n\nOn the eigenspace of ,\n. Thus any\n restores positivity in this\neigendirection, and since higher eigenvalues are larger, coercivity holds on\nall of . The general Gårding estimate below performs the same\ncorrection without diagonalizing the operator. It uses only Young's\ninequality and the coefficient bounds to find an explicit  that works\nuniformly (Evans, 2010, pp. 318–319; , Section 8.1).\n\nThe full bounded divergence-form bilinear form adds the first-order terms\n and the zeroth-order term\n to the principal form.  At the operator-valued level the\ncoefficients are bounded maps on ; multiplication operators give the\nlocal  coefficient fields.\n\nLower-order terms also change what should count as forcing. The natural dual\nof  is , whose elements need not be\nfunctions, so the loads are allowed to contain first derivatives of \nfunctions from the start.\n\nData  define the continuous functionalwhich represents the distribution  in\n.\n\nWhether such loads exhaust the dual space is a question about the Riesz\nrepresentation on the graph space. The answer is affirmative: the coordinates\nof a Riesz representative are exactly data , so nothing\nis lost by stating the shifted problem only for generalized loads.\n\nEvery continuous functional on our  graph space has a\nrepresentation  with all components in .\nThe chosen components additionally satisfy an exact squared-norm identity\nwith the dual norm.  The components are coordinates of a Riesz representative\nand need not be derivatives of one another.\n\nExtend the functional isometrically to the ambient base-gradient product,\ntake its Riesz representative, and read the base and first-derivative\ncoordinates as .  The product-space norm decomposition\ngives the squared-norm identity.\n\nThe predicate below combines the full form, the generalized load, and a mass\nshift  into one distributional equation. The shift is the parameter that\nwill be adjusted to restore coercivity;  is the original unshifted\nproblem, whose solvability is the Fredholm question.\n\nFor a measurable domain,  coefficients , , and , a\nshift , standard  data , and\n, the predicate requires  to be open and\nrecordsfor every . This is the weak form of\n.\n\nWhether the shifted form is coercive depends on the size of , and\nGårding's inequality quantifies the loss caused by the lower-order terms. It\nbounds the full quadratic form from below by half the principal ellipticity,\nminus an explicit multiple of the  norm.\n\nIf the principal form has ellipticity constant , the constants\n are nonnegative, each first-order operator has norm at most\n, and the zeroth-order operator has norm at most , thenHere  is the Dirichlet form. This is\nGårding's estimate (Evans, 2010, pp. 318–319) for operator-valued coefficients.\n\nBound each first-order pairing by Cauchy–Schwarz and Young's inequality,\nsum over coordinates, bound the zeroth-order pairing by its operator norm,\nand combine the result with principal ellipticity.\n\nRead with the mass term moved to the left, Gårding's inequality says that\n dominates  together\nwith\n.\nChoosing the shift so that the second coefficient equals  makes\nboth graph-space coordinates appear with the same constant.\n\nWe choose the explicit mass shiftThis choice records exactly which estimates were used and leaves coercivity\nconstant .\n\nSince a larger shift only adds a nonnegative multiple of\n, the same lower bound persists for every\n. The graph-space norm squared is the sum of the base and\ngradient contributions, which is why the constant  needs no\nPoincaré inequality and no bounded domain.\n\nEvery  makes the shifted full form coercive on the graph-space\n, with coercivity constant .\n\nAdd the mass shift to Gårding's inequality, decompose the graph-space norm\ninto its  and gradient components, and use monotonicity in .\n\nCoercivity of the shifted form is exactly the hypothesis of Lax–Milgram, so\nthe shifted problem is uniquely solvable for every generalized load. For\npointwise  coefficients the operator bounds  are the\n norms of  and , and boundedness of  is not\nrequired, in contrast with the unshifted principal problem.\n\nLet  be open and measurable. Suppose the principal\n coefficient matrix has ellipticity constant , while\n, every coordinate satisfies\n, and\n.\nForevery standard  load  has a unique weak solution\n.\n\nThe Gårding estimate at the explicit threshold is strengthened by the\nnonnegative extra mass term when  is larger. Lax–Milgram then gives the\nunique Hilbert-space solution, and the smooth-test density bridge identifies it\nwith the concrete shifted divergence-form weak equation.\n\nUniqueness makes the solution a function of the data, and at the canonical\nshift  that function can be named. The definition lives at the\noperator-valued level, where only integrated ellipticity and the operator-norm\nbounds are available.\n\nLet , , and  be bounded operators on .\nGiven integrated ellipticity for  and the nonnegative lower-order bounds\nused above, the canonical coercive shift determines a named Lax–Milgram\nsolution in  for every standard graph-space load\n. This definition is operator-valued and needs only\nmeasurability of ; the pointwise multiplication specialization and\nopenness enter in the distributional-PDE theorem.\n\nThe named solver is characterized by the variational identity it satisfies\nagainst every , not only against smooth tests.\n\nFor every , the named solution satisfies the full\nshifted variational identity, with right-hand side\n. The identity explicitly expands the\ngraph-space load but still permits bounded, possibly nonlocal coefficient\noperators. After specialization to multiplication operators on an open\ndomain, the smooth-test bridge gives Evans's local distributional equation.\n\nApply the weak equation for the named Lax–Milgram solver to , then expand\nthe generalized load. This gives the mass-shifted bilinear form on the left\nand  on the right.\n\nTesting this identity with  and using the coercivity constant\n at the canonical shift yields an a priori bound with a fully\nexplicit constant.\n\nUnder integrated ellipticity with constant  and the stated\nnonnegative lower-order operator bounds, the canonical operator-valued\nsolution obeys the explicit estimateThe constant is explicit for the chosen graph-space norm.\n\nTest the solver equation with . Coercivity at the canonical shift gives\n on the left, while Cauchy–Schwarz\nbounds the generalized load by\n. Cancel the solution norm to obtain the displayed\nfactor .\n\nFor local  multiplication coefficients the shifted problem also\nsatisfies weak comparison\n(weak comparison). Under the integrated\nellipticity and lower-order bounds above, fix the same coefficients and shift\n for two weak solutions . If their dual\nloads obey\n for every almost-everywhere nonnegative\n, then  almost everywhere on .\n\nTest the difference equation with . The supported Sobolev\nclosure is preserved by this operation, and\n almost everywhere, including on\n. Local multiplication then gives\n. Load order makes this quantity nonpositive;\ncoercivity forces . This localization is why comparison uses\nmultiplication coefficients even though the solver permits nonlocal operators.\n\nAt the canonical shift, the scalar-load comparison theorem specializes this\nargument to ordered scalar  loads with the divergence forcing held\nfixed. Comparison needs only measurable : here the zero-boundary\nspace means the closure of supported smooth functions. On open domains this\nis the usual . Neither boundedness nor a boundary atlas is\nrequired; the conclusion is an almost-everywhere inequality between weak\nsolutions.\n\nComparison also gives a route to a nonlinear equation. Let  now be\nbounded and measurable, and suppose ordered weak lower and upper solutions\n are given for .\nSuppose  is continuous, has at most linear growth, and\n is increasing for some real . Choose\n. The shifted reaction  is\nthen increasing, so the map\n\n\n\npreserves order. Weak comparison and the barrier inequalities give\n and\n. Starting at the lower barrier produces\n\n\n\nThe barriers bound this sequence in , and the linear growth estimate bounds\nthe shifted loads there. The linear solver bounds the iterates in ;\nRellich compactness then supplies an  limit. Monotonicity makes the\nwhole sequence converge, and continuity of the solver also gives convergence\nin . At the fixed point the two mass terms cancel, leaving\n.\n\nThe monotone iteration theorem\ncarries out this construction with the same rough multiplication coefficients\nas comparison. Consider . It is increasing, so we\ncan take ; the estimate  supplies linear growth.\nIts slope becomes unbounded near zero, but continuity is enough to pass to\nthe limit in . Zero is a lower barrier, and any nonnegative weak upper\nbarrier encloses a solution. Globally Lipschitz reactions, including\n, are special cases with  equal to the Lipschitz constant.\n\nOften the barriers give a bounded range  for every iterate. Then we\nonly need continuity of  and monotonicity of  on that interval.\nLet  and extend the reaction by\n\n\n\nOn  this is exactly . The shifted function\n is increasing and bounded, so  has\nlinear growth on the whole line. The preceding construction applies, and the\nbarriers keep its solution where the extension agrees with .\nThe interval-reaction existence theorem\nformalizes this argument. For instance, it handles  on \nwithout a global growth bound for the cubic. Both barriers belong to the\nzero-boundary space throughout this construction.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Energy and Weak Boundary-Value Problems\u0009Weak Elliptic Problems\u0009Lower-Order Divergence-Form Problems","header":"5.4.2.5.1. Lower-order terms and coercive shifts","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Energy-and-Weak-Boundary-Value-Problems--Weak-Elliptic-Problems--Lower-Order-Divergence-Form-Problems--Lower-order-terms-and-coercive-shifts"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem":{"contents":"\n\n constructed global finite-energy weak\nsolutions in three dimensions. Smooth solutions are known locally for smooth\ndata, but no theorem rules out a singularity at a later time for every\nadmissible datum. The open question is whether a smooth three-dimensional flow\ncan concentrate strongly enough to lose regularity.\n\nWhat is formalized here is the statement of the problem and two proved facts.\nThe statement is Fefferman's pair of real-domain alternatives (A) and (C), two\nLean propositions over the classical solution class; the open problem asks for\na proof of one of them. The proved facts are an abstract\ncontinuation alternative for norm trajectories, which takes the model-specific\ncontinuation criterion as a hypothesis, and the zero datum, which satisfies the\nexistence predicate of alternative (A) at every positive viscosity. The scaling\ncalculation and the regularity theory recalled first are background.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009The Three-Dimensional Regularity Problem","header":"8.7.1. The Navier–Stokes Millennium Problem","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--The-Three-Dimensional-Regularity-Problem--The-Navier___Stokes-Millennium-Problem"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Compactness-and-Lower-Semicontinuity":{"contents":"A convex functional that is lower semicontinuous in norm is weakly lower\nsemicontinuous. Strict convexity adds uniqueness; existence does not need it.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.2. Compactness and Lower Semicontinuity","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Minimization-and-Variations/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Compactness-and-Lower-Semicontinuity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Multipliers___-slices___-and-coordinate-changes":{"contents":"Derivative-word extraction is a bounded linear map from a higher-order graph\nspace to a lower-order slice.  This is the common interface used by\nhigher-order embeddings, traces of derivatives, regularity assembly, and\nmultiplier arguments.\n\nThe weak-calculus layer includes:\n\n* smooth multiplication and Leibniz rules, including preservation of\n without global bounds on the smooth multiplier;*  multiplication as a bounded bilinear map,\nwith an explicit dimension-dependent operator-norm bound;*  multiplication as a bounded bilinear map\nfor every positive integer , with an explicit recursive\ndimension-and-order dependent bound;* first-order pullback under controlled coordinate changes;* higher-order pullback under bounded  charts with  inverses,\nfor every positive integer  and ;* rowwise norm estimates for transformed weak derivatives;* assembly lemmas that recover a higher-order graph element from compatible\nderivative coordinates.\n\nThe first-order  multiplier is both a fixed-coefficient\ncontinuous linear endomorphism and a bounded bilinear map, with the constant\n for PDEAtlas's finite-product graph norms. At\nevery positive order , the  multiplier is also a\nfixed-coefficient\ncontinuous linear endomorphism and a bounded bilinear map on .\nIts recursive constant starts from the first-order bound and multiplies, at\neach successor order, by\n. This follows from contractive\nderivative-word extraction, the two Leibniz summands in each coordinate, and\nthe quantitative successor-order assembly estimate.\nThe chosen product commutes with restriction to every positive lower\nSobolev order, agrees with the dedicated first-order multiplier at order\none, and exposes the expected first-derivative Leibniz coordinates almost\neverywhere.\nThe second-order coordinate theorem retains explicit local estimates.\nThe higher-order construction instead proves boundedness through the closed\ngraph theorem after identifying the weak derivatives by induction.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.11. Multipliers, slices, and coordinate changes","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Multipliers___-slices___-and-coordinate-changes"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Certified-Numerical-Enclosures":{"contents":"The numerical command\n\n-- Enclose every value on the interval,\n-- rather than sampling points.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]] (cos x)\n\n\nreturns\n\n#cas [enclose x in [-1 / 10, 1 / 10]]\nDomain: x ∈ [-1/10, 1/10] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (cos x)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [0.995004, 1]\nUpper bound: 1\nAbsolute bound: 1\nCenter and radius: ≈0.997502 ± 0.002498\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe output bounds every value of cosine throughout the input interval. The\ndecimal preview rounds outward; the certificate retains exact rational\nendpoints. It also implies the coarser bound\n\n\n\n\n\nThe figure uses a rational function to show why a valid enclosure can still\nbe loose. Interval arithmetic treats repeated occurrences of the variable\nindependently, losing the relation between numerator and denominator.\nSubdivision restricts that loss on each smaller panel. The cosine query\nhas the same obligation: its bounds must contain every value between\nthe endpoints, however the evaluator finds them. The figure illustrates the\nmechanism; the displayed theorem is the guarantee for the cosine calculation.\n\nFor a concrete example of that dependence loss, consider\n on . Write , so .\nBounding numerator and denominator separately gives .\nTo obtain the sharper upper bound, retain their common . Since\n,\n\n\n\nTogether with nonnegativity this proves , with equality\nat . A valid interval can be wider than the actual range; a\nfailed request for a tighter interval need not mean that the requested bound\nis false. It may require a different representation or subdivision.\n\nReturning to cosine, the coarser enclosure can be stated as an ordinary theorem\nabout the one-dimensional Euclidean space and closed by cega:\n\nopen CAS.Certificate in\nexample :\n    EnclosedOn (fun p : ℝ^1 ↦ Real.cos (p 0))\n      {p | -1/10 ≤ p 0 ∧ p 0 ≤ 1/10} (716402999/720000000) 1 := by\n  cega [enclose x in [-1/10, 1/10]] (Real.cos x)\n\n\nThe rational interval computation supplies the candidate endpoints. The\ntheorem-facing finite-box API exposes the same guarantee through\nCAS.CEGA.encloseBox? and the returned attempt's\nEnclosureAttempt.sound; the more general encloseOn? API accepts\nan arbitrary semantic domain with a proved interval environment. A theorem can\nlater consume the resulting certificate without knowing which interval\nalgorithm produced it.\n\n-- Import the certificate vocabulary used\n-- by the theorem statement.\nopen CAS.Certificate\n\n-- CEGA exposes the certificate's stored soundness theorem.\nexample\n    (domain : Set ℝ)\n    (certificate : EnclosureCert ℝ domain) :\n    EnclosedOn certificate.fn domain\n      certificate.lo certificate.hi := by\n  cega using certificate\n\n\nThose endpoints are exact rationals. The proposition bounds a real-valued\nfunction, while the endpoint arithmetic uses rational numbers. The certificate\nconnects that arithmetic to the function. Endpoint representations and\nchecking costs are discussed in the Zulip threads\nInterval arithmetic -- what approach?\nand\nLeanCert.\nHere subdivision and an explicit precision option control how much work the\nenclosure calculation performs.\n\nThe same box computation also proves the other numerical claims. Below the\ncertified interval the report gives an upper bound, a bound on the magnitude,\nand the midpoint of the interval with its radius as a uniform error:\n\n-- One certified interval and the bounds\n-- read off it.\nset_option linter.hashCommand false in\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]] (x - 2)\n\n\n#cas [enclose x in [0, 1]]\nDomain: x ∈ [0, 1] ⊆ ℝ\nInput (scalar map ℝ → ℝ): (x - 2)\nOutput (certified interval in ℝ): [-2, -1]\nUpper bound: -1\nAbsolute bound: 2\nCenter and radius: -3/2 ± 1/2\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.6. Certified Numerical Enclosures","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Using-Verified-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Certified-Numerical-Enclosures"}});